 "path": "continual_arc_task_efce8044_notes.md",
 "content": "# Continual-ARC notes: task-efce8044\n\nSolved one instance. Likely task family: denoise a grid containing several large, solid rectangular color regions against a dominant background, with isolated speckle pixels in unrelated colors. The output removes extraneous isolated pixels and restores the underlying region/background color. Where a rectangular region has a small interruption/hole or missing boundary cells, infer the rectangle's intended extent from its constant-color neighboring rows/columns and complete it. Do not indiscriminately remove all non-background colors: preserve coherent filled rectangles, including small ones. Examples showed this applies to all regions, not just the most obvious large shapes. In the solved 14x21 case, colors 5 and 6 form intended rectangles on background 8; stray 0/1/2/9 cells outside those rectangles were replaced by 8, and anomalies embedded in rectangular objects were restored to the object's color. A key pitfall: some apparent spots within noisy areas are actual rectangles, so identify extent/consistency before cleaning.\n"
}
